{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prf-genus-from-hecke-completeness","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proof","doco:Section"],"label":"prf:genus-from-hecke-completeness","kind":"proof","title":"Proof of genus from Hecke completeness","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/lem-genus-from-hecke-completeness"],"text":"genus-from-hecke-completeness-proof.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"prf:genus-from-hecke-completeness\"></a>\n<strong>Proof.</strong> <em>Proof of genus from Hecke completeness</em></p>\n<p><strong>Proof.</strong>\nThe braiding on <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">V_1^{\\otimes n}</code> in <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathrm{Rep}(\\mathrm{SU}_q(2))</code> gives <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_n(q)</code>. For <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n = 2</code>: <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code> irreps. For <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n = 3</code>: <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code> irreps. For <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">n \\geq 4</code>: the embeddability constraint <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(m-1)(s-1) \\leq 2g</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">m \\geq 4</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">m &#x3C; s</code>, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\gcd(m,s) = 1</code> contributes no new torus knots at genus <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g \\leq 5</code> beyond <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{3,4}</code> (which equals <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{4,3}</code>). So the relevant Iwahori--Hecke algebra is <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_2(q) \\oplus H_3(q)</code>, with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">5</code> irreps total.</p>\n<p>A torus knot <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{r,s}</code> with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\gcd(r,s) = 1</code> has Seifert genus <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(r-1)(s-1)/2</code>, so <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,s}</code> has genus <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">(s-1)/2</code> for odd <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{3,s}</code> has genus <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s-1</code> for <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">s</code> prime to <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code>. Counting those of genus at most <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g</code>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g</code></th>\n<th align=\"left\">from <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r = 2</code></th>\n<th align=\"left\">from <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r = 3</code></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">N(g)</code></th>\n<th align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\geq 5</code>?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{2,3}, T_{2,5}</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\">---</td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">3</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">+\\,T_{2,7}</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{3,4}</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\">No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">4</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">+\\,T_{2,9}</code></td>\n<td align=\"left\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">+\\,T_{3,5}</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><code class=\"language-math math-inline\">6</code></td>\n<td align=\"center\"><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Since <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">N(3) = 4 &#x3C; 5 \\leq 6 = N(4)</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">N</code> is non-decreasing, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g = 4</code> is the least genus at which <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">N(g) \\geq 5</code>. <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\square</code></p>\n<p><strong>What this proof does not establish.</strong> An earlier version closed instead with <em>\"at <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g = 4</code>: <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">6</code> evaluations span <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">5</code> irreps (verified numerically: the <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">6 \\times 5</code> matrix has rank <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">5</code> for all <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q > 1</code>), therefore <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g_{\\min} = 4</code>.\"</em> That step is false, and no value of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> can repair it. A torus knot <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">T_{r,s}</code> is the closure of a braid word on <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r</code> strands, so a row with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r_i = 2</code> is supported in the two <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_2(q)</code> columns and a row with <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r_i = 3</code> in the three <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(q)</code> columns. The character matrix is therefore block-diagonal, and rank is additive over the blocks. At <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g = 4</code> the four <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r = 2</code> rows contribute at most <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code> (there are only two such columns) and the two <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">r = 3</code> rows contribute at most <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">2</code> (there are only two such rows), so</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">\\mathrm{rank}(M) \\leq 2 + 2 = 4 &#x3C; 5</code></pre>\n<p>independently of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>. See <a href=\"#rem:torus-evaluation-block-rank\">the block-rank remark</a>.</p>\n"}
